{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 / mathlib formalization of Dana Scott's 1980 Oxford lectures, published May 1981 as PRG-19 Lectures on a Mathematical Theory of Computation. The Palomar compared result is only the first sentence of Theorem 8.8: the neighbourhood system U over [0,1) ⊆ ℚ is universal, in the sense that every countable neighbourhood system D embeds as a subdomain (D ⊴ U). Challenge.lean fixes the neighbourhood-system vocabulary, Scott's ⊴, and the concrete Definition 8.7 family U. This foundational universality result supplies a concrete host for all countable domains; it is relevant to domain theory, denotational semantics, mathematical logic, and formalized mathematics. The broader Lectures I–VIII development lives in Scott1980/Neighborhood/* and is not the advertised Comparator result. The informal account is arxiv.md; the working transcription is sources/PRG19.md. Lean was written by AI agents under the direction and review of the author. Dana Scott did not participate in or endorse this formalization. There is no sorry outside the deliberate holes in Challenge.lean, no project-defined axiom, and Mathlib is the only Lake dependency.",
